WEBVTT

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.640
The dodo.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.120
Comical, ugly, ridiculous... and extinct.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:34.040
The most celebrated example of how human beings can wipe out other species.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.600
But the dodo is not alone.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.880
It is part of an ongoing process.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:48.800
For right now, the Earth is in the middle of a man-made mass extinction.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:59.640
No species is safe.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.560
But it doesn't have to be that way.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:11.200
It is within our power to stop this disaster.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:30.640
And if we don't, it could be we all end up like the dodo.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:55.760
When you look at the Earth teaming with life, it seems absurd to think that it's teetering on the brink.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:06.320
But life is fragile, and several times over the last 500 million years, the Earth has been struck by catastrophes that have wiped out most of the life on the planet.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.320
They call them mass extinctions.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:18.720
If the causes have sometimes been mysterious, in others, the killer left plenty of evidence.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:42.160
65 million years ago, the Earth was struck by an asteroid.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:03:02.760
The fire storm and nuclear winter that followed killed off the dinosaurs and wiped out half the species from what had seemed to be a stable world.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:26.520
Catastrophic as it was, it wasn't the worst mass extinction to strike the Earth.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.680
For that, you need to go back even further in time.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:48.680
Looking through the fossil record, palaeontologists have identified five such extinction events.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:58.080
The most devastating happened at the end of an era called the Permian, 250 million years ago.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.200
This is the dead zone.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:04.240
This zone represents what it was like here after the mass extinction.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.800
We have never found anything that represents life in this zone.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:14.440
There is no evidence of plant life, there is no evidence of soils and especially there is no evidence of animals.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.960
This is completely dead.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:30.840
Never in the history of the planet was there a catastrophe that was so widespread, so devastating, so all-inclusive.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:37.600
We were going from this very rich place to a biological desert.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:43.640
From a place like a rainforest, to a place that is composed of a few species, eking out an existence.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:53.280
95% of the species on the planet were wiped out.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:05:02.720
The probable source of this devastation didn't come from outer space, but from within the planet itself.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:11.320
Today, Siberia is an icy wasteland, but 250 million years ago, the whole area caught fire.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:24.800
You'd get a huge eruption and another and another, and maybe a lull, and then another bunch of ten, and then another.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.880
All together over a few hundred thousand years.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:34.800
The Earth is almost continuously spewing out lava until after a million years or so, millions of cubic kilometres have erupted.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:44.320
So this a truly gigantic volcanic object - thousands, tens of thousands of times larger than anything man has ever seen.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:54.760
The first effect of the eruption was to make the Earth much colder.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:06:01.040
First, we will enter a winter, a volcanic winter.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:12.280
The dust and the sulphur will cool the atmosphere after each eruption for years, or tens of years.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:26.040
But the volcanoes also spat out huge amounts of carbon dioxide, which led to a devastating process called global warming.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:39.600
The carbon dioxide will lead to a greenhouse effect and the cold will be replaced by unusual hot time.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.280
So you get a double hit.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.960
You are cold for a while.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.920
And then hot for a longer time.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:50.520
..So that, all together, after a million years, the Earth would be different.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.920
The climate would be different and a mass extinction would have happened.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:03.480
Here is the evidence of how serious global warming can be.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:11.240
But the ominous thing is that, as we speak, the Earth is going through a sixth mass-extinction event.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:16.440
One that is happening faster than any of the disasters of the past.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:32.640
The extinction rate is at crisis proportion, perhaps 100 to 1,000 times higher than before humanity came along.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:42.280
We see pretty clearly that we could lose a quarter of the species on Earth in the next several decades.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:49.200
If nothing were done, perhaps half of the species by the end of the century.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:53.520
That's a crisis.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:08:03.200
But this crisis hasn't been caused by a meteorite or a volcano or any natural disaster.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:09.320
This one is entirely down to one cause... homo sapiens.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:13.760
Wherever we go, extinction inevitably follows.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:20.160
It's not surprising, given the way we behave.

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:54.600
It seems the worst thing any species can do is to come into contact with people.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.280
And that, of course, is the big lesson of the dodo.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:12.320
The dodo is the classic example of man-made extinction.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:22.680
It all happened 400 years ago on the paradise island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:30.240
Today, it seems an unlikely place for a mass murder.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:37.680
But these excavations are the first to look for dodo bones in 120 years.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:46.320
And in the mud, they're finding the remains of hundreds of the extinct birds.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:59.600
What they're revealing is helping to paint a picture of Mauritius before man arrived.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:10.160
Julian Hume is a palaeontologist at the Natural History Museum.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:16.720
A world expert on dodos, and the environment they lived in.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:20.160
This is Mauritius as it may have been before man arrived.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:26.360
As you can see, it's very very different from what we imagine the place to be today.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:34.640
Today, sugar cane is everywhere, all of these forest tree species have gone and, unfortunately, most of the animals as well.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:43.960
Before humans arrived, it would have been dominated by things like giant tortoises, found in their thousands.

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.480
Other reptiles as well.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:55.800
Skinks, this big terrestrial species here, snakes as well.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:11:02.440
And in this very special environment we had one very special bird, that is, of course, the dodo.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:12.480
It would have been a key species in that original environment.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:17.040
A mile walk through Mauritius would probably have turned up 50 to 100 birds.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:26.280
But then, in 1598, everything changed.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:31.560
Humans arrived.

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:32.680
Sailors from Holland.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:37.040
And the slaughter began.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:56.400
The minute man arrived on Mauritius, the dodo unfortunately was doomed.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:03.440
These were desperate men, scurvy was rife, many were sick, they were starving.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:09.760
When they arrived on the island, there were only two thoughts going through man's mind.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.880
One - can they catch it?

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:15.080
And two - can they eat it?

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:20.160
We know for a fact that men ate dodos.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:28.720
There are reports of them being taken on board 20 or 50 at a time.

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:30.400
But dodo was not the sought-after gain.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:35.440
We know that some of the mariners who tried the meat absolutely despised it.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:38.600
They said it was greasy, and fatty, and totally inedible.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:43.120
The more they boiled it, the worse it became, the tougher it became.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:51.680
So it is a myth that the dodo was eaten to extinction.

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.880
But tasting foul was not enough to save it.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:13:00.640
For humans brought other less direct but more devastating changes to the island.

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:07.920
Unfortunately, animals were introduced, exotic animals that had never been found on Mauritius before.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:18.200
These included cats, deer, goats, pigs and the worst culprit of all, and inadvertently escaped from ships, was the rat.

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:32.200
These animals spread through the forests, ran wild, and started competing with dodos for their food source, probably attacking eggs and chicks, and also disturbing the nesting cycle of these birds.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:33.920
All the time, humans were confined to the coast.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:38.120
They probably weren't seeing dodos at all.

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:45.800
The warfare against the dodo and the other species on Mauritius was taking place the whole time within these unoccupied areas of Mauritius.

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:55.520
Within 80 years of humans and their deadly entourage setting foot on Mauritius, the dodo was gone.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:14:00.840
A symbol of man's power to affect the planet.

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:15.840
But the dodo wasn't the beginning of this sixth great mass extinction.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:21.560
It's been going on as long as modern humans have wandered the Earth.

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:27.240
Once, huge land animals roamed the wild places of the Earth.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:39.680
10,000 years ago, great herds of woolly mammoths strode across North America.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:46.400
Then, humans showed up.

00:14:47.480 --> 00:15:04.440
In Australia 40,000 years ago there were giant lizards and wombats the size of horses.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:10.440
But they didn't last long after people arrived.

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:20.800
And in New Zealand, giant flightless birds called moas survived untroubled until just 1,000 years ago.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:23.200
And then it happened again.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:54.200
On each continent, within just a few hundreds years of arriving, these small groups of primitive hunters wiped out all the large animals with little more than spears and bows and arrows.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:16:11.200
Whilst there is no hard and fast evidence that humans are to blame, there doesn't seem to be any other reasonable explanation.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:24.160
The one correlation around the world is that the extinctions happened whenever humans arrived.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:28.240
Let me give an analogy.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:45.280
Suppose there were 37 unsolved murders in Los Angeles, and you can establish that each of those 37 murders happened just after a certain person, John Smith, entered a building.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:53.840
That would probably be enough circumstantial evidence to convict John Smith of murder even though we didn't see him shooting the gun at each of those 37 people.

00:16:52.920 --> 00:17:10.400
On the same grounds, the fact that these extinctions around the world happened whenever humans arrived in the area, arriving in Australia 40,000 years ago, in the Americas 13,000 years ago, in New Zealand 1,000 years ago, that, I think, is enough to convict humans.

00:17:09.599 --> 00:17:20.520
One of the only places you will find large land animals now is in Africa where they evolved alongside humans.

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:27.960
Elephants and rhinos have learned how to live with, and avoid, people.

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:34.840
But even these long-term survivors are now under threat.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.920
Just look at what is happening to the hippos.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:46.880
Up until recently, they were reasonably abundant.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:52.920
But they've made it onto the Red List, the list for endangered animals.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:53.640
The reason for that is...

00:17:52.760 --> 00:18:00.480
In the Democratic Republic of Congo they'd the second-largest population in the world, there were 30,000 hippos living in that country.

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:05.680
Within the space of ten years, 95% of that population were wiped out for meat and for ivory.

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:10.040
So, within a decade, that population was decimated.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:18.200
And it's not just the large animals any more.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:21.160
Almost everything is suffering from the effects of humans.

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:23.280
And it's getting worse.

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:39.440
We are now seeing rates of extinction of maybe 50 to 150 species per day are disappearing off the face of the Earth.

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:41.600
That is far in excess of anything we have seen historically.

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:53.320
In the recent Red List, they were showing that 25% of all mammals on the planet are endangered, one in three amphibians will go, one in eight birds.

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:56.160
So these are things we are accustomed to seeing and they're highly endangered.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:19:08.640
The current rate of extinction is even faster than the Permian.

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:11.000
Of course, there is a school of thought that says we really don't have anything to worry about.

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:18.080
They say that nature is all about competition and extinction is just a natural part of that process.

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:39.840
Natural-history museums the world over are monuments to animals that have lost their battle with nature.

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:47.320
Some of them were the greatest ever to walk the Earth.

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:56.080
We have a fossil record which goes back for no less than 3,000 million years.

00:19:55.480 --> 00:20:00.520
It tells us beyond any question that species always eventually go extinct.

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:05.240
It is a normal, inevitable part of the process of evolution.

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:15.280
We don't know what percentage of the species which ever lived are still alive today but 1%, maybe 1 in 1,000, as few as that.

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:17.800
All the rest have gone extinct.

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:29.600
Over 99% of all of the creatures that have ever lived are now extinct.

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:33.240
And most of them never came into contact with humans.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:38.800
Even the top dogs, the species which look like they'd go on for ever, were all eventually snuffed out.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:47.880
Let me give you an example.

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:55.840
Up to about 200 million years ago, there was a group of animals called the mammal-like reptiles.

00:20:55.280 --> 00:21:01.960
They were far and away the dominant terrestrial animals walking the surface of the Earth.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:07.160
This is an example of one of these creatures which was clearly carnivorous.

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:14.120
You can see the wonderfully sharp teeth and the big canines.

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:22.520
Its main prey would have been things like this animal here - the herbivores of the time.

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:30.080
They would be living in big herds, they would be grazing, a little like the sort of mammals one would see in the savannah in Africa today.

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:34.480
The scene would look rather like that.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:39.960
One would have supposed they would last for ever.

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:48.440
By about 200 million years ago, a new group had appeared, which we call the dinosaurs.

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:56.120
They seem to have been better adapted at life on land.

00:21:55.160 --> 00:22:04.960
And, relatively quickly, the mammal-like reptiles had disappeared, gone extinct, and the dinosaurs had taken over.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:13.000
The lesson from this would seem to be that extinction just happens.

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:16.040
And there is nothing anyone can do about it.

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:30.480
There are a few exceptions - creatures which have survived for hundreds of millions of years.

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:37.560
But there seem to be no common features that explain why they're still here.

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:55.040
Horseshoe crabs have been virtually unchanged for 300 million years.

00:22:54.120 --> 00:23:07.400
They've survived the Permian disaster, the asteroid that killed the dinosaurs, climate change, ice ages and, so far, humanity.

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:13.440
Scientists have spent years trying to find out if they can teach us about survival.

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:17.600
But with no success.

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:23.200
We can perhaps say that it was its adaptability, its resistance to predation, which allowed it to survive.

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:29.800
Quite why it managed to survive the environmental changes over this long period of time is, I have to say, really quite a mystery.

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:36.680
So perhaps, in the natural course of events, extinction and survival is just a matter of chance.

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:48.480
There are patterns in this current extinction that suggest there is more than just luck at work.

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:54.440
That some animals are particularly vulnerable to human activity.

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:09.160
And one factor is clearer than any other.

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:27.840
A role call of extinctions over the last 400 years tells a sorry story - Madeiran wood pigeon, Canary Islands giant rat, Puerto Rican shrew, the Hawaiian honey creeper, Samoan wood rail, the Javan tiger, Seychelles parakeet, the Cyprus dipper.

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:38.760
All of these creatures lived on islands quite happily number the arrival of humans.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:42.920
In fact, the isolation probably helped them survive.

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:51.120
But island life is also the evolutionary equivalent of moving to a gated retirement community.

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:57.640
Once you have been there for a while, you lose the ability to cope with the outside world.

00:24:56.960 --> 00:25:01.280
Once again, the dodo is the perfect example.

00:25:00.520 --> 00:25:07.760
The dodo's forebears looked like this Nicabar pigeon when they first arrived in Mauritius.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:13.000
A million years of evolution later and that pigeon was completely unrecognisable.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:18.120
The wings had all but gone, the beak had grown, and the chest had puffed out.

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:18.440
Today, it looks comic.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:25.920
But the dodo was perfectly adapted to its island home.

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:31.160
Why would something like the dodo lose the ability to fly?

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:40.200
Well, actually, to produce flight is very expensive in terms of energy for birds.

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:58.200
Now, imagine an ancestral pigeon landing on somewhere like Mauritius, an isolated volcanic island, no predators, all the food it requires is on the ground, it doesn't need to fly.

00:25:57.240 --> 00:26:07.960
The bird could get fatter, store more weight and, over time, evolve into this giant ground pigeon.

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:12.960
The dodo was beautifully adapted to its island home.

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:16.840
But once humans arrived, it couldn't adapt quickly enough to the changes they brought.

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:22.600
The dodo had become specialised.

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:28.040
Predators from elsewhere arrive on the island, the dodo cannot cope.

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:34.560
It cannot change millions of years of evolution in a very short time.

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:40.200
It's a story that has been retold on islands around the world.

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:48.880
More than half of the species that have become extinct in modern times lived on islands.

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:52.080
..Until they were visited by humans.

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:56.400
Many of them, like the dodo, were flightless birds.

00:26:55.680 --> 00:27:01.640
Most oceanic islands had one or two species.

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:04.880
Now, they are almost all gone.

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:13.120
Some, like this Laysan rail from Hawaii, survived into the 20th century.

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:15.680
Before they scuttled off into extinction.

00:27:16.120 --> 00:27:24.080
But even birds that can fly perfectly well are not safe.

00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:37.000
The island of Guam once had a thriving bird population until, in the 1940s, brown tree snakes were imported on boats.

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:40.720
The birds had no idea what to do when confronted with the snakes.

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:44.960
Rather than fly away, they just sat there.

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:52.760
Today, over 80% of Guam's forest bird species are extinct.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:28:02.120
But the most famous human-caused bird extinction didn't happen on an island, it happened in America.

00:28:01.520 --> 00:28:06.120
It was the passenger pigeon.

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:08.640
The easiest target in the world.

00:28:09.320 --> 00:28:16.360
The decline of the passenger pigeon is the most dramatic decline of any creature we know of.

00:28:15.800 --> 00:28:31.280
At the start of the 19th century, it was probably the commonest bird on Earth.

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:39.760
The birds lived in enormous flocks of many millions.

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:48.440
They blackened the sky, made so much noise, you couldn't even hear a man shouting, so many wings were beating in the sky.

00:28:47.560 --> 00:28:54.720
These enormous flocks would cause immense destruction to crops and trees, anything that got in the way.

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:57.640
They were a plague of locusts.

00:28:57.440 --> 00:29:03.960
So when passenger pigeons arrived in a particular area, people were fairly horrified.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:04.440
They were incredibly easy to shoot.

00:29:03.640 --> 00:29:12.240
You'd get a gun and just aim it the sky, and you just pull the trigger and you bring down passenger pigeons every time you shot.

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:23.640
Most birds would instinctively fly away from the hunters' guns, but not the passenger pigeon.

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:30.000
In fact, it was impossibly easy to attract the birds.

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:41.440
You would catch a passenger pigeon, tie it to a pole and its squawks would attract the other birds.

00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:45.680
The decoys had a name - they were called stool pigeons.

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:49.000
They lured millions of their species to their deaths.

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:56.440
We are quite savage beasts.

00:29:55.480 --> 00:30:02.560
The thought of killing, say, 5,000 birds in a couple of afternoons does not disturb many people.

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:12.080
It may disturb you, me, it may disturb thousands of others but it doesn't disturb everybody.

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:28.760
Such was the combination of the pigeons' suicidal nature and the hunters' savagery, that within just 50 years, at the end of the 19th century, their numbers declined from six billion to just one individual.

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:35.760
A fall in population of 250,000 birds every day.

00:30:36.400 --> 00:30:45.400
When the numbers drop below a certain level, even though that level may have been many, many millions, it was doomed, even though it was still incredibly common.

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:50.920
The numbers just spiralled down and down and down until the only remaining birds were a few that had been captured.

00:30:49.960 --> 00:30:58.560
The last one was called Martha, named after George Washington's wife.

00:30:57.680 --> 00:31:05.280
She died in September 1914 in the Cincinnati Zoo.

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:13.640
The final straw for the poor old passenger pigeon was that once it was in trouble, it couldn't breed fast enough to keep its numbers up.

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:23.640
The speed with which animals reproduce can have a huge effect on what species survive and which fall to extinction.

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:37.640
Some species can wipe out others just by having sex.

00:31:36.720 --> 00:31:46.480
In 1859, humans introduced just 24 rabbits to Australia.

00:31:45.680 --> 00:31:47.680
No-one meant it to happen.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:52.280
But 100 years later, there were 600 million of them.

00:31:55.200 --> 00:32:01.560
It was colonisation by sheer force of numbers.

00:32:00.680 --> 00:32:05.720
Many native species were lost but it was a triumph for the rabbit.

00:32:08.080 --> 00:32:18.680
In contrast, the inability to breed effectively is currently sounding the death knell for many creatures.

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:24.400
..Most notably the panda.

00:32:24.200 --> 00:32:29.800
Human pressure has already exposed its vulnerability.

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:34.320
Fragmenting its habitat and decimating its numbers.

00:32:33.560 --> 00:32:41.880
But the heart of their problem is that they seem strangely unsuited to survival.

00:32:42.320 --> 00:32:45.520
It starts with their diet.

00:32:44.720 --> 00:32:50.160
The giant panda is an absolute curious beast.

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:50.720
It's a bear.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:57.280
By all rights it's a carnivore but it spends all of its time eating bamboo.

00:32:56.520 --> 00:33:02.040
That's not a particularly highly calorific food stuff.

00:33:01.160 --> 00:33:10.880
In fact, it's estimated that pandas only get 10% of the goodness out of the bamboo it eats because its digestive system is not designed to digest bamboo.

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:14.400
What that means is that they have to spend a huge amount of time eating.

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:25.000
A fully-grown panda can eat anything from 15 to 50 kilos of bamboo a day, which is up to half of its bodyweight in a day.

00:33:24.040 --> 00:33:27.000
This is not a productive use of its time.

00:33:26.240 --> 00:33:34.680
And because it's so low in energy, they tend to be lethargic, so they spend most of the time just sitting down and eating.

00:33:33.760 --> 00:33:35.800
And it's not just their diet.

00:33:35.040 --> 00:33:40.560
Pandas are simply terrible at sex.

00:33:39.800 --> 00:33:43.280
They just don't have the urge.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:56.440
They're not the best lovers and reproducers in the world.

00:33:55.760 --> 00:34:05.520
They can't learn unless they see other pandas having sex, because they're solitary.

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:11.040
The issue comes actually for the panda to understand how to have sex.

00:34:10.280 --> 00:34:13.800
What positions you adopt in order to be successful and so on.

00:34:13.080 --> 00:34:19.679
Unless they have exposure to learn that somehow, I think it will always be problematic.

00:34:19.440 --> 00:34:26.199
And to make it even worse, they're very rarely fertile.

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:40.800
The females only come into heat once a year, normally in about April, in the spring, there's a window of 3-4 days when she can actually conceive.

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:46.199
In the period leading up to that, both sexes tend to become more vocal.

00:34:45.239 --> 00:34:51.920
They scent mark a lot more to excite one another.

00:34:50.960 --> 00:34:58.440
But even doing those things, in many cases, either they don't have sex at all or, if they do, it may not be successful.

00:34:57.760 --> 00:35:06.720
It may seem hard to blame humans for the decline of a creature that seems so ill-adapted to survival.

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:13.920
In fact, humans have been trying their best to help pandas breed.

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:22.000
We've made them watch panda pornography!

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:27.760
And have slipped Viagra into their food.

00:35:27.920 --> 00:35:35.080
More recently, IVF methods have been adapted so that pandas don't have to make any effort at all.

00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:46.280
And it's been a resounding success.

00:35:45.440 --> 00:35:49.440
In the Chinese sanctuaries, baby pandas are being popped out like champagne corks.

00:35:53.080 --> 00:35:55.640
Typically, no-one is more surprised at the success than the pandas themselves!

00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:56.040
And this is the most bizarre element of the present mass extinction.

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:11.400
Human beings are not just being destructive.

00:36:10.600 --> 00:36:21.120
This is the only mass extinction where the cause of death is also trying to undo what it has done.

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:28.040
So is it possible that we could reverse the whole extinction process?

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:43.160
One of the main ways to stave off extinction is captive breeding programmes.

00:36:42.200 --> 00:36:53.960
Around the world, zoos like London Zoo dedicate a lot of their time and money to saving some of world's most endangered species.

00:36:55.160 --> 00:37:01.880
And it's not just the glamorous species that are being saved.

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:17.600
There are probably more endangered species in this room in terms of number of individuals within those species than there is in any comparable space on the planet.

00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:25.200
They're all Polynesian tree snails, from French Polynesia.

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:31.320
They're part of a group of snails called partula snails.

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:39.920
The partula tree snails were forced towards extinction by a series of human-related catastrophes.

00:37:39.040 --> 00:37:45.360
50 years ago, the partula were the only snails in Polynesia.

00:37:44.680 --> 00:37:58.080
But then humans introduced giant African land snails.

00:37:57.320 --> 00:38:00.920
The theory was that they would be good to eat.

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:03.640
Unfortunately, they weren't.

00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:09.920
And before anyone knew it, they were everywhere.

00:38:11.120 --> 00:38:19.520
In an attempt to control the plague of snails, yet more snails were introduced.

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:23.200
This time, the cannibalistic rosy wolf snail.

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:26.200
And that was the second disaster.

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:36.320
They have these receptors, these long moustache on each side of the snail's head which they can wave about.

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:44.000
We called them fu manchu snails because they have a long... moustache.

00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:51.720
The trouble was that fu manchu didn't want to eat the giant African snails.

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:56.040
They did develop a taste for the natives partulas.

00:38:55.200 --> 00:39:04.280
They can travel several times faster than your normal snail can, so they can find these snails very efficiently.

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:05.360
They are very good hunters.

00:39:04.600 --> 00:39:11.520
They can eat their way through an estimated eight partulas a day.

00:39:10.760 --> 00:39:15.680
You can imagine the impact they actually had.

00:39:15.480 --> 00:39:19.200
Before long, we were seeing whole species becoming wiped out.

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:27.600
Luckily, some people were alert to the partula's plight.

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:34.320
As the carnivorous snails swept across the island, a rescue operation swung into action.

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:46.480
In some cases, we would get to ends of valleys, these high volcanic valleys and then find that the actual predator was just sweeping through the last areas of the parts where you found the snails and we would collect the last handful.

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:54.320
It was the last possible moment where they could be rescued.

00:39:53.360 --> 00:40:05.160
Saved from their island paradise, the snails have been nurtured and bred in these bare glass tanks for the last 15 years and now they are thriving.

00:40:08.560 --> 00:40:17.080
Rather than just a handful of snails, now there are enough of some of the species to fill a bucket.

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:21.280
Enough to guarantee their survival.

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:30.800
But not every snail could be brought back from the brink, like poor partula turgida.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:36.280
Unfortunately, on 1st January 1996, turgida was found dead in the morning.

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:40.200
I was the one who saw it unfortunately.

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:43.760
Despite our best efforts, they just fizzled out.

00:40:43.480 --> 00:40:46.920
They didn't respond to the breeding effort.

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:48.360
So, it's obviously very, very sad.

00:40:48.320 --> 00:40:50.720
You've done what you could, you gave it a chance.

00:40:51.920 --> 00:41:00.440
The snails had been lucky enough to get their chance but captive breeding can only be part of the answer.

00:41:00.880 --> 00:41:08.080
There's just not room in the world's zoos to house all of the endangered species...

00:41:07.200 --> 00:41:09.880
..Especially the bigger ones.

00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:17.520
We may have to put the future of some species on ice.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:22.560
This is a frozen arc.

00:41:21.720 --> 00:41:24.200
This one is in London Zoo.

00:41:23.320 --> 00:41:32.560
A few skin cells frozen in here may protect a species from extinction for ever.

00:41:31.720 --> 00:41:44.360
As long as some precious DNA is preserved, it may one day be possible to bring the animals back to life by the power of cloning.

00:41:44.280 --> 00:41:47.000
In fact, it's already happening.

00:41:46.240 --> 00:41:50.000
This is no ordinary calf.

00:41:49.120 --> 00:41:55.600
It's a rare banteng from Java and it's a clone.

00:41:54.840 --> 00:42:04.440
A clone made from the cells of animal that died in San Diego Zoo in 1980.

00:42:03.560 --> 00:42:08.640
This is Jurassic Park stuff.

00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:11.120
If we can clone animals that have been dead for more than 20 years, how much further back could we go?

00:42:10.200 --> 00:42:13.960
Could we even clone animals that are already extinct?

00:42:13.240 --> 00:42:23.520
Could the DNA preserved in dodo bones be used to bring them back to life 400 years after the last one died?

00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:25.880
Sadly, there are limits.

00:42:25.040 --> 00:42:31.120
They're not going to clone a dodo.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:34.880
It's not possible to clone a dodo.

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:42.080
It seems that despite the science fiction, the quality of DNA from dodo bones just isn't good enough.

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:49.480
Ancient DNA is preserved best in bones that are preserved in very cold and very dry conditions.

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:54.480
The warmer the environment, the more difficult it is to recover DNA.

00:42:53.840 --> 00:42:55.240
We can only get very, very small fragments.

00:42:54.360 --> 00:42:58.160
For the dodo, we were only to get one hundred or so bases at a time.

00:42:57.400 --> 00:43:03.760
Our genomes have more than three billion bases in them.

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:21.760
In order to clone the animal, you would have to be able to extract everything in those one hundred base fragments, figure out how to piece them together, figure out how to wrap them up, figure out where they go, figure out how to get them into the nucleus and then find an actual host that would be usable to develop this extinct animal.

00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:29.680
There is an argument that there would be no point reviving creatures that have already died out.

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:31.080
Why would we want to do it?

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:31.280
Where are they going live?

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:47.240
What is the point in bringing back an animal whose habitat is destroyed, who don't have any others of the same species to associate with, so we can bring back and stick them in a zoo, walk on by and wave at it.

00:43:46.480 --> 00:43:47.600
What is the point of all of this?

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:51.800
Shouldn't we be spending this money actually protecting the species that are still alive?

00:43:50.920 --> 00:44:02.200
So, for the foreseeable future at least, human efforts will largely be confined to the species that are still alive.

00:44:01.520 --> 00:44:10.880
The trouble is that this scientific effort is often undone by those with far baser motives.

00:44:14.680 --> 00:44:23.720
Because, there are those who think animals are worth more dead than alive.

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:31.240
The illegal trade in wildlife is the third largest illegal trade in the world.

00:44:30.480 --> 00:44:36.720
Drugs being first, illegal arms and armaments second, and then the illegal wildlife trade.

00:44:36.080 --> 00:44:42.440
It is estimated between 5-110 billion dollars a year.

00:44:41.480 --> 00:44:45.800
It's a massive trade.

00:44:45.120 --> 00:44:50.080
It's a trade that is growing every year.

00:44:53.800 --> 00:45:01.400
This shawl comes from the Tibetan antelope, or the chiru.

00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:08.520
They kill the antelope and pluck the fine wool off the skin.

00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:13.080
This will sell between £1,500 to £2,000 retail in the United Kingdom.

00:45:12.200 --> 00:45:17.960
It's been hunted at a level of 20,000 animals a year.

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:22.760
It's estimated there are only 80,000 animals left now.

00:45:22.040 --> 00:45:24.200
This is a rhino horn... made into a dagger.

00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:29.560
The handle here is made of rhino horn.

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:33.800
This is why the rhino was poached to near extinction.

00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:35.920
Ivory.

00:45:35.360 --> 00:45:44.360
You can tell that it's ivory purely from the smoothness that you have.

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:49.120
You can tell from some of the yellowing that goes on that it's a genuine ivory item.

00:45:48.440 --> 00:45:49.760
If I show you on a tusk.

00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:55.080
You can see where it matches the curvature of the ivory tusk.

00:45:55.840 --> 00:46:01.120
A monitor lizard, it's been made into a handbag.

00:46:01.240 --> 00:46:05.480
You can see how tasteless this item is completely.

00:46:04.680 --> 00:46:11.560
If you open it up, you can actually see the back legs as well.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:20.840
Hundreds of species are affected by the trade in wildlife parts.

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:27.240
There is one animal that is suffering more than most.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:39.200
Such is the demand for tiger parts that it has been pushed to the brink of extinction.

00:46:40.400 --> 00:46:55.960
On the Thames, just next to MI6, another group of spooks is monitoring the trade in tiger parts, and what they're finding is that it's getting worse.

00:46:55.200 --> 00:46:57.840
Because of the internet.

00:46:59.520 --> 00:47:04.160
The internet is easy, it's cheap and anonymous.

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:11.040
It makes it very attractive for people to buy and sell products, especially if they might be illegal products.

00:47:10.240 --> 00:47:18.840
One of the biggest problems is the high-tech end of a very old-fashioned business.

00:47:18.040 --> 00:47:23.200
Traditional Chinese medicine.

00:47:22.320 --> 00:47:26.200
A key ingredient of which is tiger bone.

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:31.440
Here we've got hu gu tonic.

00:47:30.520 --> 00:47:34.840
Hu gu is the pinion for tiger bone.

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:45.200
It's willing to sell you four packs for $126 and you can click to "add to cart", and then you are committing an offence, you are buying an endangered species.

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:48.880
The demand for tiger bone products is almost insatiable.

00:47:48.280 --> 00:47:59.760
A single seizure of bones in Taiwan contained the remains of 5% of the world's Sumatran tigers.

00:47:58.840 --> 00:48:12.120
If the trade in tigers continues as it does now, tigers could be extinct within the next 15 to 20 years, by the next generation.

00:48:11.240 --> 00:48:14.320
It's very, very sobering thought.

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:20.360
We have to act now if we are to stop them disappearing from the wild for ever.

00:48:25.840 --> 00:48:31.440
So this is where humanity has got to.

00:48:30.680 --> 00:48:45.000
We are at a crossroads.

00:48:44.320 --> 00:49:04.440
If we carry on as we are now, cutting down the forests, polluting the rivers, introducing alien species around the world, and pumping filth into the atmosphere, then the mass extinction will continue.

00:49:04.120 --> 00:49:16.440
But, uniquely, we have it in our power to reverse the process.

00:49:16.120 --> 00:49:18.800
The issue is do we have the will?

00:49:17.840 --> 00:49:26.160
Because if we don't, it is possible that we too will join the ranks of the extinct.

00:49:25.400 --> 00:49:34.000
It doesn't matter how intelligent we are, if things get beyond a certain point, nothing will save us.

00:49:46.240 --> 00:49:53.760
I think human beings are a failed species.

00:49:52.920 --> 00:49:56.800
We are on the way out.

00:49:55.840 --> 00:49:58.000
We are flawed.

00:50:00.640 --> 00:50:11.520
Our intelligence isn't of the kind that is compatible with nature.

00:50:10.680 --> 00:50:14.640
We are living too wrong.

00:50:13.920 --> 00:50:18.280
We are breaking all the rules.

00:50:17.440 --> 00:50:28.680
Our lives are so artificial that they can't possibly be sustained within the limits of our particular planet.

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:36.320
Like any species that's got something wrong with it, nature is going to get the better of it and see to it that it becomes extinct.

00:50:38.080 --> 00:50:44.840
It's an extreme view.

00:50:43.880 --> 00:50:56.840
But Professor Boulter believes we are stretching the planet's ability to support us and that we will wipe ourselves out, just as we once did to the dodo.

00:50:56.280 --> 00:51:08.600
Because it is possible that we are not going be able to cope with the changes we are forcing on the Earth.

00:51:09.240 --> 00:51:17.880
There are twice as many of us as there were 50 years ago.

00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:22.920
We are all consuming more, warming the planet.

00:51:22.120 --> 00:51:30.520
Unless we stop, the equatorial regions will turn into deserts.

00:51:30.360 --> 00:51:43.600
And as sea levels rise, there will be less and less land to support us.

00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:45.360
What will we eat?

00:51:44.800 --> 00:51:47.520
Where will we grow it?

00:51:49.560 --> 00:51:54.280
With famine comes war.

00:51:53.880 --> 00:51:57.680
And they are followed by plague and pestilence.

00:51:58.240 --> 00:52:05.480
As more and more people cram into a smaller space, disease will spread.

00:52:04.520 --> 00:52:08.440
We will need medicine but where will we get it from?

00:52:08.800 --> 00:52:17.400
Many medicines are derived from tropical plants.

00:52:16.520 --> 00:52:22.320
As they are swallowed by the desert, where will we turn?

00:52:21.760 --> 00:52:25.440
It could all be leading to disaster.

00:52:27.960 --> 00:52:33.680
It might seem far-fetched, but it has happened before.

00:52:32.880 --> 00:52:42.880
History is littered with civilisations that collapsed because of the damage they did to the environment.

00:52:42.840 --> 00:52:53.680
The Maya thrived in the jungles of Central America, until they reached the point where the land could support them no longer.

00:52:54.920 --> 00:53:02.360
Easter island was once covered in forest.

00:53:01.440 --> 00:53:08.920
But people cut down the trees to help build their statues.

00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:14.880
Only when they were all gone did they realise they couldn't live without them.

00:53:14.360 --> 00:53:24.160
These statues stand as a testimony to what happens to those who destroy their environment.

00:53:28.600 --> 00:53:39.200
I think it would be a grave injustice to speak of the human species as in some sense evil.

00:53:38.320 --> 00:53:42.600
Even though we are destroying the environment so efficiently at the present time.

00:53:43.960 --> 00:53:47.960
Basically, that's not our intent, it never was.

00:53:47.000 --> 00:54:02.680
It was necessary for survival for the ancestral human being to throw everything they had against the wilderness in an attempt to conquer it, to utilise it, to open it, for new populations.

00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:11.480
And for millions of years, that paid off without undo damage to the environment.

00:54:10.600 --> 00:54:17.440
But then what happened was we succeeded too well.

00:54:16.480 --> 00:54:17.840
And at long last we broke nature.

00:54:19.120 --> 00:54:31.560
If we are unable to undo the damage we have caused, some think that the best thing that could happen to the planet is for humans to die out.

00:54:30.680 --> 00:54:38.640
The planet would, of course, be delighted for humans to become extinct!

00:54:38.760 --> 00:54:41.160
The sooner it happens the better.

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:46.600
There's no question that the planet would go wild with celebration.

00:54:45.720 --> 00:54:51.040
The other species would be pleased to be rid of us.

00:54:50.440 --> 00:55:00.120
It might seem like a glum future for humans but perhaps not for the planet as a whole.

00:54:59.360 --> 00:55:03.560
Without us, life would still go on.

00:55:16.680 --> 00:55:21.320
It's in times of dramatic catastrophe that extinctions take place.

00:55:21.560 --> 00:55:35.840
It's there that there are opportunities for evolution to start new groups, within the new environmental opportunities that catastrophe has given.

00:55:38.720 --> 00:55:54.840
One of the great things about life is its resilience and with humans out of the way, life could bounce back remarkably quickly.

00:55:54.880 --> 00:56:07.240
After a few years, the seas will start to fill with fish again.

00:56:06.320 --> 00:56:21.160
The forests will start to regrow and as our cities crumble, the air will be filled with bird song once more.

00:56:20.360 --> 00:56:26.800
Within just a few centuries, the planet will forget about us.

00:56:27.240 --> 00:56:30.120
A natural order will be restored.

00:56:37.520 --> 00:56:53.280
There was some work I was involved in some years ago, where we found a new continent.

00:56:53.640 --> 00:57:04.640
35 million years ago, this new continent suddenly emerged out of the sea.

00:57:04.680 --> 00:57:22.720
And it lasted until about 10 million years ago and then suddenly the North Atlantic got wider and this continent submerged again.

00:57:25.160 --> 00:57:39.840
There are no mountains, there is no connection to any other land, but there are plants, seeds and things that have blown there, there were birds and there are other invertebrates living there.

00:57:39.240 --> 00:57:44.320
It must have been a very pleasant, peaceful place.

00:57:46.840 --> 00:57:55.360
It's that concept that I often think the Earth is trying to attain.

00:57:54.600 --> 00:57:59.280
That is the kind of environment it wants for itself.

00:57:58.920 --> 00:58:07.120
And I think that's kind of place that it will turn back to once the present catastrophe has passed.

00:58:09.240 --> 00:58:18.040
That's the way the Earth will be again - just as nature intended.

00:58:18.080 --> 00:58:23.160
It might be a lovely place, just we won't be there to see it.

00:58:22.360 --> 00:58:26.160
Unless we change our ways.
